  (W, "Boolean operators have a subtle behaviour that is easy to miss. If either value combined by and is False the whole expression is False, and if either value combined by or is True the whole expression is True. For the expression False and spam it does not matter whether spam is True or False, because the answer is already False; Python uses this fact to skip the right-hand side altogether. This shortcut is called short-circuiting [SW]. The reference states the rule exactly: x and y evaluates x first and, if x is false, returns it, otherwise evaluates y [PSF]."),
